WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.920
Pull up a chair. This is Models and Agents Episode 95 for June 28, 2026.

00:00:05.640 --> 00:00:09.300
Let's see what happened in the AI world today, and trust me, it's been busy.

00:00:09.880 --> 00:00:15.380
The week's biggest developments pulled together, what actually moved, why it matters, and what to watch next.

00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:21.000
Openweight releases from Chinese labs kept landing with little warning while open AI

00:00:21.000 --> 00:00:25.400
pushed multiple GTT updates aimed at security and agent workflows.

00:00:26.120 --> 00:00:32.120
Inference techniques such as block diffusion and two tower designs showed measurable throughput gains

00:00:32.120 --> 00:00:37.420
on existing hardware. Agent memory frameworks and environment simulating models

00:00:37.420 --> 00:00:43.720
appeared alongside internal reports of longer running codex tasks inside open AI itself.

00:00:44.780 --> 00:00:49.720
GLM 5.2 arrived as the latest openweight release from a Chinese startup.

00:00:50.180 --> 00:00:54.420
Multiple outlets report the model is attracting attention in Silicon Valley

00:00:54.420 --> 00:00:59.940
for its performance relative to current open families. The release continues the pattern

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:05.720
of rapid iteration from labs such as those behind Quen and DeepSeek, where new weights appear

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:11.520
with minimal fanfare and quickly circulate through developer communities. Early coverage notes the

00:01:11.520 --> 00:01:17.340
model is being tested for both research and agent workloads, though concrete benchmark numbers and

00:01:17.340 --> 00:01:23.380
context length details remain sparse in initial reports. Builders working on cost-sensitive

00:01:23.380 --> 00:01:29.240
inference or fine-tuning experiments now have another candidate to benchmark against llama and

00:01:29.240 --> 00:01:35.300
mistral derivatives. Watch for fast inference integrations and any follow-on fine-tunes that

00:01:35.300 --> 00:01:41.260
typically appear within days of these releases. Remember the ongoing story of openweight models

00:01:41.260 --> 00:01:45.820
narrowing the gap with closed frontier systems that we tracked through last week.

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:52.820
GLM 5.2 moves that arc forward by giving developers another immediate option without

00:01:52.820 --> 00:02:01.560
licensing friction. OpenAI expanded its daybreak program with GPT 5.5 cyber, codex security,

00:02:02.200 --> 00:02:09.000
and patch the planet. The release includes a full GPT 5.5 cyber model for tracing vulnerabilities,

00:02:09.780 --> 00:02:16.100
validating issues, and generating patches. The codex security plugin runs deep scans and exports

00:02:16.100 --> 00:02:22.380
to existing tools. It also adds a cyber partner program and direct work with maintainers via trail

00:02:22.380 --> 00:02:28.840
of bits and hacker one. The stack targets authorize defensive work only and keeps human review

00:02:28.840 --> 00:02:35.140
at the center of every remediation. Teams can now move from findings to merged fixes inside the

00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:41.120
same environment they already use for code. Watch for how security vendors integrate the new model

00:02:41.120 --> 00:02:45.540
through the partner program and whether open source projects see faster patch turnaround.

00:02:46.160 --> 00:02:52.320
This fits the frontier models thread we have followed since episode 94, where capability gains

00:02:52.320 --> 00:02:59.700
continue alongside tighter governance rules. UC San Diego's D Flash replaces standard speculative

00:02:59.700 --> 00:03:06.160
decoding with a lightweight block diffusion model that drafts entire token blocks in one forward pass.

00:03:06.980 --> 00:03:12.960
It conditions on target model hidden features via KV injection and ships with 20 checkpoints plus

00:03:12.960 --> 00:03:24.200
support for SGLANG, VALA-LM, and TENSER-RT-HIFEN-LLM. The approach reports up to 6.08 times lossless speedup

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:31.660
on Quinn 38B and NVIDIA measured peaks of 15 times throughput on Blackwell hardware at fixed

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:37.880
interactivity. Builders working on high throughput inference pipelines can now test the integration

00:03:37.880 --> 00:03:43.940
without custom drafting logic. Watch for community benchmarks on other model families and whether

00:03:43.940 --> 00:03:50.040
the block diffusion drafting generalizes beyond the reported setups. That development lands squarely

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:55.680
in the AI compute and inference program we covered last week, where the focus remains on moving the

00:03:55.680 --> 00:04:03.460
cost curve without new silicon. Quinn released a 35B parameter mixture of experts model with only about

00:04:03.460 --> 00:04:09.780
3B active parameters per token. The model was trained to predict environment states rather than

00:04:09.780 --> 00:04:15.860
generate chat responses directly. Two additional agent world variants followed each focused on

00:04:15.860 --> 00:04:23.260
simulating tool, terminal, and GUI interactions. Developers can now fine tune or prompt these

00:04:23.260 --> 00:04:29.260
checkpoints to improve agent planning loops before deployment. The release keeps the open-weight

00:04:29.260 --> 00:04:34.720
family's conversation moving, testing whether environment specific pre-training closes more

00:04:34.720 --> 00:04:43.760
of the reliability gap on long horizon tasks. Open AI released an updated GPT 5.5 instant that improves

00:04:43.760 --> 00:04:49.820
intent understanding behind questions and adapts responses more reliably to complex constraints.

00:04:50.520 --> 00:04:56.380
The update also makes shopping and local recommendations more cohesive. It is rolling out today to

00:04:56.380 --> 00:05:02.840
paid users and tomorrow to free users. Builders should test prompts that previously produced inconsistent

00:05:02.840 --> 00:05:09.060
outputs on multi-constraint tasks to see whether the new version reduces follow-up clarification turns.

00:05:09.920 --> 00:05:15.400
Watch how the update interacts with agent workflows that chain multiple tool calls.

00:05:16.040 --> 00:05:22.020
Open AI stated that agents powered by codecs are now transforming work across every department,

00:05:22.020 --> 00:05:27.600
handling tasks that are more complex, longer running, and increasingly cross-functional.

00:05:28.120 --> 00:05:35.020
The company positions this internal usage as an early signal of how agentic tools will reshape workflows

00:05:35.020 --> 00:05:40.760
once they reach broader availability. No specific benchmarks or model versions were shared,

00:05:41.240 --> 00:05:47.120
but the emphasis on cross-functional scope suggests agents are moving beyond single user chat interfaces

00:05:47.120 --> 00:05:53.580
into multi-step, multi-stakeholder processes. Builders should note the implied requirements around

00:05:53.580 --> 00:05:59.040
reliability, state management, and tool interoperability that such deployments demand.

00:05:59.700 --> 00:06:05.620
Nvidia introduced Nemotron 2 Tower, a block-wise auto-regressive diffusion model built on

00:06:05.620 --> 00:06:14.080
Nemotron 3 Nano 30B A3B. The architecture splits context handling from diffusion denoising and

00:06:14.080 --> 00:06:20.640
delivers 2.4 two times throughput at near parity quality. The design targets the same inference

00:06:20.640 --> 00:06:28.020
economics questions we tracked earlier in the week. Open AI announced the GPT 5.6 family with

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:36.200
soul as the new flagship, Terra for competitive performance at half the cost of GPT 5.5 and Luna as

00:06:36.200 --> 00:06:42.020
the lowest cost option. The family ships with a strengthened real-time safety stack that includes

00:06:42.020 --> 00:06:50.460
human red teaming and over 700,000 A100 equivalent GPU hours of testing. Soul sets a new state of the

00:06:50.460 --> 00:06:57.540
art on terminal bench 2.1 for complex command line workflows. The models introduce explicit cache

00:06:57.540 --> 00:07:04.880
breakpoints and a guaranteed 30-minute cache lifetime build at 1.25 times the uncashed input rate.

00:07:05.640 --> 00:07:11.340
The models are currently restricted to a limited preview for roughly 20 trusted partners after open

00:07:11.340 --> 00:07:16.780
AI shared plans with the US government. Broughter releases expected in the coming weeks.

00:07:17.180 --> 00:07:22.440
Builders gain clear tiered choices for intelligence versus cost on long horizon tasks.

00:07:23.140 --> 00:07:29.860
Anthropic regained limited US government approval to redeploy its Mythos 5 cybersecurity model

00:07:29.860 --> 00:07:36.720
to critical infrastructure operators. A new MR agent framework cuts long horizon agent memory

00:07:36.720 --> 00:07:43.880
costs dramatically versus Langmem. Simon Willison shipped the ski-light-dashed utles version 4 release

00:07:43.880 --> 00:07:49.660
candidate, adding built-in migrations and nested transaction support for developers working with

00:07:49.660 --> 00:07:56.160
local data. Temporary cloud flare accounts now let agents spin up ephemeral workers deployments in

00:07:56.160 --> 00:08:01.940
under a minute without an account. The seven types of agent memory guide and a detailed matrix

00:08:01.940 --> 00:08:07.940
recurrent units update provide concrete engineering references for builders. Naga translate

00:08:07.940 --> 00:08:14.360
demonstrates a full translation and voice pipeline for low resource nagaland creoles built on whisper,

00:08:14.740 --> 00:08:21.880
vits, and large language models. If you work with local databases the SQL ETU 12 release candidate

00:08:21.880 --> 00:08:26.520
is worth installing this week to test the new migration commands on a copy of your data.

00:08:26.520 --> 00:08:31.720
Developers exploring agent environments can pull the quen agent world checkpoints for

00:08:31.720 --> 00:08:37.600
model scope and run a short terminal simulation loop. Anyone testing inference speedups should add

00:08:37.600 --> 00:08:45.040
the deflash integration to an existing sglang or vllm setup and measure block level throughput on

00:08:45.040 --> 00:08:52.760
blackwell hardware. Keep an eye on devday 2026 in San Francisco on September 29 where the keynote will

00:08:52.760 --> 00:09:00.160
be live streamed. Brotter access to the GPT 5.6 family is also expected in the coming weeks.

00:09:01.020 --> 00:09:04.900
Okay let's pop the hood on the inference techniques that surface this week.

00:09:05.560 --> 00:09:10.840
Deflash moves beyond token by token speculative decoding by training a lightweight block

00:09:10.840 --> 00:09:17.540
diffusion model that proposes entire blocks in a single forward pass. KV injection from the target

00:09:17.540 --> 00:09:23.420
model hidden states conditions the draft removing the need for separate drafting logic at runtime.

00:09:24.100 --> 00:09:32.880
The reported 6.08 times lossless gain on quen 3.8 b and 15 times peak on blackwell comes from

00:09:32.880 --> 00:09:37.360
immortalizing the cost of the diffusion step across many tokens at once.

00:09:38.340 --> 00:09:43.620
Nemotron 2 tower takes a different route by decoupling auto regressive context in coding

00:09:43.620 --> 00:09:50.680
from diffusion based denoising in a two tower layout. The split allows the context tower to run once

00:09:50.680 --> 00:09:57.600
while the diffusion tower iterates only on the denoising task producing the measured 2.42 times

00:09:57.600 --> 00:10:03.460
throughput at near parity quality. When deciding between these approaches reach for deflash if you

00:10:03.460 --> 00:10:12.340
already run sglang or vllm and want plug-in speedups without retraining. Reach for two tower designs

00:10:12.340 --> 00:10:18.600
when your workload benefits from explicit separation of context and generation steps on Nvidia hardware.

